    "project_template_level_name": "aif-y2-making-predictions-lesson6-choice3-2026 + template",
    "long_instructions": "Sasha gives you a CSV file of data on other salons. \"Here’s how many monthly customers other salons have,\" they say. \"Could a model predict how many mine will have?\"\r\n\r\n- `population` is the population of the salon’s town, in thousands.\r\n- `salons` is the number of *other* salons already open in the town.\r\n- `weekends` is whether the salon is open on weekends.\r\n- `type` is the type of salon.\r\n- `customers` is how many monthly customers the salon gets.\r\n\r\n**☑️ Do This:**\r\n\r\n- Under `# TODO: Encoding`, encode the features your model will use. Each encoding should be a variable that stores a list of feature values from the data.\r\n  - The data from the CSV file is given to you in `data` as a list of dictionaries—one per row.\r\n  - Be sure to cast numeric data using `int`, because it is given to you as a string.\r\n\r\n**💻 Coding Tip**\r\n\r\nRecall you last used dictionaries in Unit 4, Lesson 4.\r\n\r\nA **list comprehension** is a way to build a new list based on an existing list. For example:\r\n\r\n```\r\nage_encoding = [int(row[\"age\"]) for row in data]\r\n```\r\n\r\nYou can also use conditionals in your list comprehension. For example:\r\n\r\n```\r\nage_encoding = [1 if int(row[\"age\"]) > 18 else 0 for row in data]\r\n```\r\n\r\nAlso remember that `OneHotEncoder` is a class from `scikit-learn`. You can use it to create and configure an object to encode your data using a one-hot encoding:\r\n\r\n```\r\nencoder = OneHotEncoder()\r\n```\r\n\r\nAnd once you’ve created an encoder, you can use it to create an encoding. Where `\"FEATURE\"` is the feature you’re encoding…\r\n\r\n```\r\nencoding = encoder.fit_transform([[row[\"FEATURE\"]] for row in data])\r\n```",
